{"tool_name":"tmkit","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"tmkit","home":"https://github.com/2003100127/tmkit","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Python interface for computational analysis of transmembrane proteins.","addition_date":"2024-01-15T13:49:12.701829Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T14:21:32.987051Z","tool_type":["Desktop application","Workflow"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:tmkit","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"adam.cribbs@ndorms.ox.ac.uk","name":"Adam P Cribbs","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0001-5288-3077","typeEntity":"Person"},{"email":"gromiha@iitm.ac.in","name":"M Michael Gromiha","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1776-4096","typeEntity":"Person"}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://tmkit-guide.herokuapp.com"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"public"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Fold recognition","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0303"},{"term":"Residue contact prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0272"},{"term":"Sequence visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0564"},{"term":"Transmembrane protein prediction","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0269"},{"term":"Transmembrane protein visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2241"}]}],"name":"TMKit","owner":"Pub2Tools","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bib/bbad288","metadata":{"abstract":"Transmembrane proteins are receptors, enzymes, transporters and ion channels that are instrumental in regulating a variety of cellular activities, such as signal transduction and cell communication. Despite tremendous progress in computational capacities to support protein research, there is still a significant gap in the availability of specialized computational analysis toolkits for transmembrane protein research. Here, we introduce TMKit, an open-source Python programming interface that is modular, scalable and specifically designed for processing transmembrane protein data. TMKit is a one-stop computational analysis tool for transmembrane proteins, enabling users to perform database wrangling, engineer features at the mutational, domain and topological levels, and visualize protein-protein interaction interfaces. In addition, TMKit includes seqNetRR, a high-performance computing library that allows customized construction of a large number of residue connections. This library is particularly well suited for assigning correlation matrix-based features at a fast speed. TMKit should serve as a useful tool for researchers in assisting the study of transmembrane protein sequences and structures. TMKit is publicly available through https://github.com/2003100127/tmkit and https://tmkit-guide.herokuapp.com/doc/overview.","authors":[{"name":"Cribbs A.P."},{"name":"Gromiha M.M."},{"name":"Kulandaisamy A."},{"name":"Ru J."},{"name":"Sun J."}],"date":"2023-09-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Briefings in Bioinformatics","title":"TMKit: a Python interface for computational analysis of transmembrane proteins"},"pmcid":"PMC10516361","pmid":"37594311"}],"topic":[{"term":"Enzymes","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0821"},{"term":"Membrane and lipoproteins","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0820"},{"term":"Protein folding, stability and design","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0130"},{"term":"Protein folds and structural domains","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0736"},{"term":"Protein interactions","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0128"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"TMKit","home":"https://bio.tools/tmkit","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Python interface for computational analysis of transmembrane proteins.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:tmkit/app/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":true}},"project":{"build":{},"